2024-11-03 11:10:38,445 - INFO - Solution: We can factor $n^2-11n+24$ as $(n-3)(n-8)$. For this quantity to be less than or equal to 0, one of the factors must be less than or equal to 0 and the other factor must be greater than or equal to 0. Specifically, since $n-8<n-3$ for all $n$, we must have $$n-8 \le 0 \le n-3.$$ The first inequality, $n-8\le 0$, tells us that $n\le 8$. The second inequality, $0\le n-3$, tells us that $n\ge 3$. The solutions to the original inequality must satisfy both conditions, so they are given by $3\le n\le 8$. The largest integer in this interval is $n=\boxed{8}$.
